//? Title: DAG Longest Paths: Finalize Predecessors, Relax Once, Recover the Route
//? Subtitle: The original length-nine diamond, negative weights, precedence timing and actual linear work
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A directed acyclic graph turns longest-path optimization into dynamic
programming: process vertices in topological order and maximize each outgoing
candidate. We retain the original four-vertex example and recover its actual
route 0→2→3 of length 9. Native traces then distinguish finite negative
paths from unreachable vertices, retain exact parallel-edge identities, and
show how strict ties depend on processing order. A precedence network derives
earliest feasible milestone times, while chain and dense DAG workloads count
the actual ordering and relaxation phases. One canonical core supplies the
legacy result, full traces and safe local witness recovery. Whole-graph cycle
checks and finite candidate additions prevent misleading partial results.
The recurrence is exact in exact arithmetic; ordinary double sums can merge
distinct real path totals, a limitation demonstrated explicitly below.
)

\toc

> Begin with the original four-vertex diamond

The edges are 0→1 with weight 2, 0→2 with weight 1, 1→3 with weight 3 and
2→3 with weight 8. From source 0, the two complete routes weigh 5 and 9.
The original report prints 9. Here the same core also exposes the order and
actual improvements: vertex 3 first receives 5 through vertex 1, then 9
through vertex 2. The chosen edge ids are 1 and 3 in the original input vector.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dag_longest_diamond.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<dm::algorithms::WeightedEdge> edges{{0,1,2},{0,2,1},{1,3,3},{2,3,8}};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::dag_longest_paths_trace(4,edges,0);const auto p=dm::algorithms::dag_longest_path_to(t,3);
  const auto old=dm::algorithms::dag_longest_paths(4,edges,0);
  if(old.distance[3]!=9 || p.vertices!=std::vector<std::size_t>{0,2,3} || p.edge_indices!=std::vector<std::size_t>{1,3}) return 1;
  std::print("| attempt | original edge | from | to | finalized tail distance | old destination | candidate | improved |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<t.relaxations.size();++i) {
    const auto& e=t.relaxations[i];const auto& edge=t.edges[e.edge_index];
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",i+1,e.edge_index,std::get<0>(edge),std::get<1>(edge),e.from_distance,e.previous_distance,e.candidate,e.improved);
  }
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(78,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=2.25;box.height=1.25;box.font_size=19;
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> positions{{0,0},{4,2.2},{4,-2.2},{8,0}};
  for(std::size_t v=0;v<4;++v) {
    box.fill=std::find(p.vertices.begin(),p.vertices.end(),v)!=p.vertices.end()?dm::graphics::RGB{220,252,231}:dm::graphics::RGB{241,245,249};
    d.node(std::to_string(v),positions[v],std::format("vertex {}\nbest {}",v,t.result.distance[v]),box);
  }
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<edges.size();++i) {
    const bool chosen=std::find(p.edge_indices.begin(),p.edge_indices.end(),i)!=p.edge_indices.end();dm::graphics::DiagramStroke stroke;stroke.width=chosen?4:2;stroke.color=chosen?dm::graphics::RGB{22,163,74}:dm::graphics::RGB{100,116,139};
    const auto& e=edges[i];d.edge(std::to_string(std::get<0>(e)),std::to_string(std::get<1>(e)),std::format("weight {}",std::get<2>(e)),stroke);
  }
  d.text({4,4.1},std::format("Actual topological order {}",t.topological_order),22)
   .text({4,-4},std::format("Chosen route {}; original edges {}; weight {}",p.vertices,p.edge_indices,p.weight),21)
   .save_svg("build/dag_longest_diamond.svg");
  std::print("\nOriginal result {}; chosen vertices {}; original edge ids {}; stored total {}.\n",old.distance[3],p.vertices,p.edge_indices,p.weight);
}
