\caption(The green edges are the recovered witness. The table records the actual two successive destination values, rather than reconstructing an imagined execution from final distances.)
\alttext(A diamond has upper route weights two and three, and lower route weights one and eight. The lower green route reaches total nine. Vertex labels show distances zero, two, one and nine.)

> Derive the recurrence and its proof

For a fixed source s, let D(s)=0 for the empty path and D(v)=−∞ initially
for v≠s. For v≠s, the exact-arithmetic longest-path recurrence is

\[
D(v)=\max_{(u,v)\in E,\;u\ \mathrm{reachable}}
\bigl(D(u)+w(u,v)\bigr).
\]
\alttext(A reached vertex receives the maximum finalized predecessor distance plus its incoming edge weight. An unreachable vertex retains negative infinity.)

A DAG admits a topological ordering: every edge goes from an earlier vertex
to a later one. Induct on that order. When u is processed, all of its
predecessors have been processed, so every path to u has already contributed
its last-edge candidate. Every path to a successor v decomposes into a path
to one such predecessor and the final edge. Taking their maximum is therefore
sound and complete. Once all predecessors of v have run, D(v) is final.
Negative edge weights do not disturb this ordering argument. Every directed
path in a DAG is simple, so cycles cannot create repeated-vertex walks.

The unrestricted simple longest-path problem is NP-hard. With unit weights,
a simple path of V−1 edges is exactly a Hamiltonian path, so a general exact
longest-path solver would decide Hamiltonian-path existence. Restricting the
input to a DAG supplies the topological order used by this dynamic program.
This implementation requires
the entire input graph to be acyclic, including components unreachable from
s. A self-loop is a cycle. Kahn's algorithm queues all zero-indegree vertices,
removes each once and decrements the indegree along each outgoing edge. If
fewer than V vertices are removed, the remaining vertices contain a cycle;
the function rejects before attempting any numerical relaxations.

Initial ready vertices are queued in increasing id. Newly ready vertices are
appended in original outgoing-edge order. This is a FIFO policy, not a
smallest-id priority queue, and uses no sorting. Relaxations use strict stored
candidate>D(v), without an epsilon. Equal stored candidates retain the first
witness established by that processing order; no lexicographically smallest
route or unique optimum is promised. Parallel edges remain separate, and
predecessor-edge ids identify which one was chosen.

> Negative paths, unreachable vertices and ties are different cases

In the next graph, routes 0→1→3 and 0→2→3 both weigh 3: −2+5 and −1+4.
The first reaches vertex 3 earlier in the actual topological order and keeps
the tie. Vertex 1 is reachable with distance −2. Vertices 4 and 5 form a
separate acyclic component and are unreachable from source 0. Their negative-
infinite distances are sentinels, rather than large negative path weights.
The source itself has a valid one-vertex, zero-edge witness of weight zero.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dag_longest_signed.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<dm::algorithms::WeightedEdge> edges{{0,1,-2},{0,2,-1},{1,3,5},{2,3,4},{4,5,9}};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::dag_longest_paths_trace(6,edges,0);const auto chosen=dm::algorithms::dag_longest_path_to(t,3);
  if(t.result.distance[1]!=-2 || t.result.predecessor[3]!=1 || chosen.weight!=3 || dm::algorithms::dag_longest_path_to(t,5).found) return 1;
  std::print("| vertex | reachable | stored distance | vertex witness | edge witness |\n| ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(70,1150);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=2.1;box.height=1.25;box.font_size=19;
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> positions{{0,0},{3.6,2.2},{3.6,-2.2},{7.2,0},{10.6,2.2},{10.6,-2.2}};
  for(std::size_t v=0;v<6;++v) {
    const auto p=dm::algorithms::dag_longest_path_to(t,v);const auto distance=p.found?std::format("{}",p.weight):"unreachable";
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",v,p.found,distance,p.vertices,p.edge_indices);
    box.fill=p.found?dm::graphics::RGB{219,234,254}:dm::graphics::RGB{241,245,249};
    d.node(std::to_string(v),positions[v],std::format("vertex {}\n{}",v,distance),box);
  }
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<edges.size();++i) {
    const auto& e=edges[i];dm::graphics::DiagramStroke stroke;stroke.color=i==4?dm::graphics::RGB{148,163,184}:dm::graphics::RGB{51,65,85};
    d.edge(std::to_string(std::get<0>(e)),std::to_string(std::get<1>(e)),std::format("{}",std::get<2>(e)),stroke);
  }
  d.text({5.3,4.2},"Negative is reachable; unreachable has no source path",23)
   .text({5.3,-4},std::format("{} edges checked topologically; {} reachable-edge candidates; tied route {} retained",t.work.topological_edge_visits,t.work.relaxation_attempts,chosen.vertices),19)
   .save_svg("build/dag_longest_signed.svg");
  const auto parallel=dm::algorithms::dag_longest_paths_trace(2,{{0,1,1},{0,1,3},{0,1,3}},0);
  const auto p=dm::algorithms::dag_longest_path_to(parallel,1);
  if(p.edge_indices!=std::vector<std::size_t>{1}) return 1;
  std::print("\nActual order {}; whole-graph vertices {}; reachable vertices {}; attempted reachable edges {}.\n",t.topological_order,t.work.topological_pops,t.work.reachable_vertices,t.work.relaxation_attempts);
  std::print("Parallel weights (1,3,3): chosen original edge {}; strict improvements {}; last equal candidate improved {}.\n",p.edge_indices,parallel.work.improvements,parallel.relaxations.back().improved);
}
