> Numerical and validation contracts matter to the conclusion

In the binary64 control, H=2^53 and the two real route totals are H and H+1.
Under the default round-to-nearest execution used here, H+1 rounds to H.
The first stored H is retained, even though its exact-real route is shorter.
This does not invalidate the topological proof; it shows the difference
between an exact-arithmetic recurrence and its rounded double execution.
Small integer fixtures with exactly representable intermediate sums avoid
this particular problem. The API does not supply exact-real comparisons,
interval enclosures or compensated path accumulation.

Every edge endpoint, finite weight and source is checked before allocation
and optional trace caps. A cycle anywhere rejects after adjacency allocation
and whole-graph ordering, before numerical relaxations. Every reachable
candidate sum must be finite; overflow to either infinity throws instead of
turning a reached path into a false unreachable sentinel. This includes an
extremely negative candidate that would lose to an already known better
route. Unreachable edges are still validated, but no candidate addition is
performed for them. Empty graphs have no valid source; a single isolated
source has the valid empty path.

The trace defaults to 100,000 vertices and 1,000,000 input edges, checked as
complete caps after full domain validation and before graph/order/history
allocation. Zero disables the corresponding optional cap. Cycle checks follow
those caps, so an oversized cyclic input may hit a cap first. No trace
silently truncates. Legacy output-only calls have no optional cap and retain
the original result layout and include path. Source and unreachable
predecessor sentinels remain V; trace edge sentinels are E.

Permanent native tests independently enumerate all paths for 202013 graph/
source cases over every increasing-id DAG through six vertices, with mixed
small integer weights and every source. A further 1000 seeded relabelled
DAGs include arbitrary id order and parallel edges. Tests replay actual
events, check topological edge directions and exact edge witnesses, and
cover negative/unreachable/tied/source cases, global cycles, complete caps,
full finite/domain validation, both overflow signs and the rounded-tie
control. Small generator shapes, overflow-safe output counts, owned and
malformed local witnesses, chain/dense counters and 100000 isolated vertices
are covered. All nine new cases pass AddressSanitizer and UBSan. These are
algorithmic and precedence-model controls, with no unrelated dataset needed.

> References and reproducibility

- R. Sedgewick and K. Wayne, Algorithms, 4th ed., section 4.4, directed
  acyclic longest paths and precedence scheduling:
  https://algs4.cs.princeton.edu/44sp/ and
  https://algs4.cs.princeton.edu/code/javadoc/edu/princeton/cs/algs4/AcyclicLP.html .
  The same authors discuss general longest-path intractability in section 6.6:
  https://algs4.cs.princeton.edu/66intractability/ .
- J. E. Kelley, Jr. and M. R. Walker (1959), “Critical-path planning and
  scheduling,” Eastern Joint IRE-AIEE-ACM Computer Conference:
  https://doi.org/10.1145/1460299.1460318 .

Every block is a complete editable C++23 program using dm/dm.hpp. The
canonical core supplies ordering, solving, actual traces, path recovery and
workload generation. Native figures are saved to local build/ and copied
into figures/; output blocks contain tables and local image references rather
than SVG XML, data URIs or base64 payloads.
